  "question": {
    "text": "Let $f(x)$ be a polynomial function of second degree. If $f(1)=f(-1)$ and $a, b, c$ are in A.P, then $f^{\\prime}(a), f^{\\prime}(c)$ are in",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "Wh2rC",
      "text": "\nArithmetic-Geometric Progression\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "sIIUe",
      "text": "\nA.P.\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "W8TFL",
      "text": "\nG.P.\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "o1DXK",
      "text": "\nH.P.",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$f(x)=a x^2+b x+c$\n$f(1)=f(-1) \\Rightarrow a+b+c=a-b+c$ or $b=0$\n$\\therefore f(x)=a x^2+c$ or $f^{\\prime}(x)=2 a x$\nNow $f^{\\prime}(a) ; f^{\\prime}(b) ;$ and $f^{\\prime}(c)$ are $2 a(a) ; 2 a(b) ; 2 a(c)$. If $a, b, c$ are in A.P. then $f^{\\prime}(a) ; f^{\\prime}(b)$ and $f^{\\prime}(c)$ are also in A.P.",
    "image": null
  },
